                  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                    Sealing a <strong class="text-white">concrete driveway</strong> comes down to three decisions: the right sealer type, a fully cured and genuinely dry slab, and thin even coats. Penetrating silane or siloxane sealers are the right default where freeze-thaw is the concern; acrylics suit decorative and stamped surfaces where appearance matters.
                  </p>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
                    Give new concrete its full 28 days, allow 24 to 48 dry hours after washing, work between 50°F and 90°F out of direct midday sun, and apply two light coats with the second at right angles to the first. Every common failure in this job — clouding, peeling, slipperiness — comes from skipping one of those.
                  </p>
